WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.089
Speaker 1: Today.

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:04.710
We're going to be speaking with Sheila Heen,
who's a Harvard Law School professor, founder

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:10.790
of Triad Consulting, and also coauthor of
the New York Times bestsellers Difficult Conversations,

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:12.420
as well as thanks for the feedback.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.000
A Sheila, it's really great to have you on
today.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.940
Speaker 5: I'm delighted to be here.

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:22.490
Speaker 1: So I've every week I get questions
from people in my audience.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:29.050
I have this very big political disagreement
with my mom and I want to know how to handle

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:30.050
it.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:34.480
Or I have this difficult situation with my
coworkers politically and I want to know how

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:35.480
to handle it.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.300
And people ask me for advice.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:41.079
And one of the first things I say is, well,
to figure out the right path forward, you

00:00:41.079 --> 00:00:44.230
kind of first have to think about what you
want the outcome to be.

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:48.480
In other words, in a political disagreement
with your mom, what's more important, proving

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.850
her wrong or maintaining a relationship with
your mom, for example, depending on what what

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:53.850
you expect might happen?

00:00:53.850 --> 00:01:00.649
So can you kind of open up by giving us a
basic framework in terms of how to think generally

00:01:00.649 --> 00:01:04.150
about some of these difficult conversations
that people sometimes have.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:05.799
Speaker 5: Have sort of way?

00:01:05.799 --> 00:01:11.749
And and David, I think you're right on the
money, meaning I think one of the core questions

00:01:11.749 --> 00:01:15.549
is, what is my purpose in having a conversation?

00:01:15.549 --> 00:01:21.549
Because if my purpose is to prove her wrong,
that that is not necessarily going to change

00:01:21.549 --> 00:01:22.549
her mind.

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:23.549
You may have noticed that true.

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:24.549
Right.

00:01:24.549 --> 00:01:30.119
Because it's totally clear to you that you
have proven her wrong and yet her views persist.

00:01:30.119 --> 00:01:31.119
Right.

00:01:31.119 --> 00:01:36.469
And so I think that's part of the struggle,
which is that I actually think that there

00:01:36.469 --> 00:01:41.639
are things that are hard about just being
in relationship with someone who holds a view

00:01:41.639 --> 00:01:47.979
that you find incredibly disturbing, upsetting,
wrongheaded, foolish, uninformed.

00:01:47.979 --> 00:01:50.890
Should we keep going?

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:51.890
Right.

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:54.079
Speaker 1: All those things.

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:55.079
Yeah, right.

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:56.079
Right.

00:01:56.079 --> 00:02:00.500
And it's important also, because there's a
range of the example I give often is when

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.099
people say, are you friends with like radical
right people?

00:02:03.099 --> 00:02:07.520
For example, I'll say, listen, if someone
thinks the tax rate should be two points lower

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.700
than I do, it's not a big deal.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:11.019
It's just a disagreement.

00:02:11.019 --> 00:02:12.400
And we can kind of just work through that.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.260
It's not really going to affect too many things
about my ability to relate to them.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:22.110
If they believe there's a secret cabal of
Jews controlling every aspect of, you know,

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:26.569
where I that's going to be a little bit different
in terms of our ability to relate to each

00:02:26.569 --> 00:02:27.569
other.

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:29.200
So it seems like that context is important,
too.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.040
Speaker 5: I think that that's exactly right
and.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:40.450
And so I think what we're talking about are
views that feel like they share at the core

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:41.450
of who we are.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:42.450
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:49.250
Speaker 5: Or that suggest a threat to something
that we hold really dear.

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:54.920
And so in this weird way, there's like an
identity issue that comes with like, how could

00:02:54.920 --> 00:03:03.109
I being who I am and what I care about and
what I want people to see about who I am and

00:03:03.109 --> 00:03:04.569
respect about who I am?

00:03:04.569 --> 00:03:05.569
How could I be?

00:03:05.569 --> 00:03:07.050
Fill in the blank.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:10.579
Friends with family, with neighbors, with
coworkers.

00:03:10.579 --> 00:03:18.969
With this person who the at least the caricature
of the view that they hold in some way invalidates

00:03:18.969 --> 00:03:25.099
dismisses or oppresses something that really
matters to me about who I am.

00:03:25.099 --> 00:03:32.610
And just that that identity quake that comes
with that question, I think is why we feel

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:34.959
like we need to have a conversation with them.

00:03:34.959 --> 00:03:35.959
Right.

00:03:35.959 --> 00:03:38.829
Speaker 1: And what's what would thinking
of techniques?

00:03:38.829 --> 00:03:42.670
You know, sometimes it'll be like, hey, we
need to talk.

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:47.689
Or You said something that really offended
me, starting with how one felt.

00:03:47.689 --> 00:03:51.989
There's all these different tactics that that
are often mentioned.

00:03:51.989 --> 00:03:55.760
Is there some universal best way to kind of
start some of these conversations, or does

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.540
it really depend on who the person is and
what you're going to talk about?

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:03.700
Speaker 5: So I think that the thing that
it depends most on is negotiating with yourself

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:04.849
about your purpose.

00:04:04.849 --> 00:04:05.849
Yeah.

00:04:05.849 --> 00:04:10.980
So for instance, the couple of examples that
you just gave, if they just said something

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:15.939
or did something that I want to raise, well
then there is a purpose to the conversation.

00:04:15.939 --> 00:04:22.680
And my purpose would be I want to share with
you sort of what impact that had for me or

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:30.020
why I found it upsetting or frustrating, or
that you're not really understanding something

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.229
that feels important to me for you to understand.

00:04:32.229 --> 00:04:33.229
Right.

00:04:33.229 --> 00:04:35.650
So my purpose is actually to share something
with you and to feel heard.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:37.449
Well, that's a purpose I might be able to
achieve.

00:04:37.449 --> 00:04:38.449
Right.

00:04:38.449 --> 00:04:39.449
Speaker 1: Right.

00:04:39.449 --> 00:04:46.000
Speaker 5: And if I've got my purpose aligned
with something that feels realistic, well,

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.199
then I can start by just sharing that purpose.

00:04:48.199 --> 00:04:53.449
Like, Hey, whether or not you agree, I want
you to understand why this matters to me.

00:04:53.449 --> 00:04:55.020
That would be a great purpose.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:59.500
And depending on the relationship, they may
or may not say like, okay, or they may start

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:00.720
explaining why you're wrong.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:01.900
Speaker 1: Right?

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:02.900
Yeah.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:08.460
You know, when I spoke to one, I spoke to
someone who has hired and fired lots of people.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.669
And I think this will be kind of parallel
to what we're talking about.

00:05:11.669 --> 00:05:15.520
And he said one of the things he doesn't like,
which he did early in his career when you

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:20.069
would have to fire someone, is he would bring
them in and he would go listen.

00:05:20.069 --> 00:05:23.310
So I want to talk because things haven't been
going so well lately.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:25.430
And then the employee might say they haven't.

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:27.060
I think things have been going fine.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.250
You're already derailed, right.

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:29.979
And you're kind of trying to back up.

00:05:29.979 --> 00:05:35.270
And so his his thing was he would go, listen,
thanks for meeting with me.

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:36.400
We're going to have to let you go.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.280
Let me explain to you why.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:42.379
And then you're putting out front that there
is a decision and there's a reality here.

00:05:42.379 --> 00:05:46.229
And now it's just a matter of kind of thinking
it through and talking it through.

00:05:46.229 --> 00:05:51.129
Is that where does that fit into the techniques
that you think can be useful?

00:05:51.129 --> 00:05:53.419
And is there something about that, too?

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:55.380
Instead of listen.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:57.430
Remember the other day when we were talking
about I think.

00:05:57.430 --> 00:05:58.599
No, I don't remember that.

00:05:58.599 --> 00:06:03.419
Just saying we we had a conversation that
left me feeling really upset with you.

00:06:03.419 --> 00:06:04.440
And I want to tell you what happened.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:05.440
I want to.

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:06.960
Is there something about that as a technique?

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:08.379
Speaker 5: Totally.

00:06:08.379 --> 00:06:09.379
Totally.

00:06:09.379 --> 00:06:10.379
So.

00:06:10.379 --> 00:06:13.430
So one of the things that you're describing
is what we would call a bad news conversation.

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:14.430
Hmm.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:19.230
Like there's something that has been decided,
or there's something that matters to me.

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:22.169
And the purpose of this conversation is partly
to let you know that.

00:06:22.169 --> 00:06:25.560
Yeah, and if the person conversations to let
you know that, then I need to put the bad

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:26.860
news upfront.

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:32.100
And what's funny is that if you ask people
if somebody has bad news for you or needs

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:37.400
to tell you something important, what what
are your requests of them?

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:38.400
Yeah.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:39.490
Almost everybody says, like, just tell me
upfront.

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:44.030
Like, don't tiptoe into it or hide the ball
or make me guess.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:48.360
But then when we're on the other side of the
conversation, we ease into it.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:51.480
I've watched someone try to fire someone by
asking them questions.

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:52.480
Speaker 3: Right.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:56.419
Speaker 5: But don't you think you'd be better
off, you know, in a different organization

00:06:56.419 --> 00:06:57.419
and.

00:06:57.419 --> 00:06:58.419
Speaker 1: Not really know.

00:06:58.419 --> 00:06:59.419
Speaker 5: It was a friend of theirs?

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:00.419
Right.

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:03.410
So we do it because because of some of the
identity stuff I was talking about.

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:05.170
Like, I'm not the kind of person who hurts
people's feelings.

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:07.580
I'm not the kind of person who treats someone
unfairly.

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:15.240
And and that's why a lot of getting ready
to have an important conversation means negotiate

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:17.150
with yourself, like, well, what's true?

00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:18.340
You might hurt their feelings.

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:19.419
Yeah, let's.

00:07:19.419 --> 00:07:23.229
Maybe you are sensitive and maybe that's okay.

00:07:23.229 --> 00:07:26.740
And you need to let them know, like, look,
this is just a topic on which I am sensitive.

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:33.979
Yeah, we don't argue about whether I'm normal,
sensitive or oversensitive or you're insensitive.

00:07:33.979 --> 00:07:36.900
But if we're going to be in relationship with
each other, this is where we're at.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:42.069
Speaker 1: One of the other situations that
often comes up in the political space, and

00:07:42.069 --> 00:07:45.280
this is less about people in my audience,
but more about some of the people that I talk

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:51.350
about politicians or public figures is allegations
are often made against people of all different

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:52.350
kinds.

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:58.180
And then often there's like an instant evaluation
of how an individual reacts when accused of

00:07:58.180 --> 00:07:59.759
something.

00:07:59.759 --> 00:08:05.009
And unfortunately, what can be difficult about
it is no matter how you react, it's sort of

00:08:05.009 --> 00:08:08.599
seen as though like, of course, that's what
being what's being said, you know, and whether

00:08:08.599 --> 00:08:11.500
the allegation is of some kind of criminality.

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:15.910
We've seen this in the context of the MeToo
movement, you know, all sorts of allegations.

00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:21.430
And if people say, I absolutely didn't do
that, the reaction can often be, well, that's

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:23.129
what everybody would say, at least initially.

00:08:23.129 --> 00:08:24.129
Right.

00:08:24.129 --> 00:08:25.129
Or most most people would.

00:08:25.129 --> 00:08:31.780
Or you kind of get the idea when there are
accusations being hurled at you.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:38.510
What are some of the ways that are better
to respond maybe than what we often see?

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:45.180
Speaker 5: Well, so I'll give an answer that
I actually learned from someone else.

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:55.060
So years ago we were doing a program in collaboration
with another group, and the other group included

00:08:55.060 --> 00:09:00.430
people who did internal investigations within
the CIA, FBI, etc.

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:06.620
And so what they were teaching was how to
read signals that someone might not be giving

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:07.620
you the straight story.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:08.620
Speaker 1: Yep.

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:13.710
Speaker 5: So one of the things that I learned
from them is that one of the most common red

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:15.980
flags is what's called a protest answer.

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:17.110
Speaker 1: Mm hmm.

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:23.310
Speaker 5: So if you accuse me of fill in
the blank, rather than saying I absolutely

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:28.250
did not do that, the person says, I can't
even believe you would ask me that.

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:29.250
Speaker 1: Mm.

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:32.410
Speaker 5: I can't even believe you would
have the nerve to, etc., etc..

00:09:32.410 --> 00:09:37.410
So what's interesting is not that if I'm a
truthful person and I didn't do what's being

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:43.290
accused, that I wouldn't be horrified that
you're even asking or suspecting me.

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:49.260
But what they want to say is, if you are innocent
and telling the truth, the truth is your friend.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:53.370
So the first thing you're going to blurt out
is the truth, which is like, of course not.

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:57.310
Then you can add, I can't even believe you're
asking me that.

00:09:57.310 --> 00:09:58.310
So.

00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:01.940
So what's interesting is that you feel if
you're on the, the being accused side of it,

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:04.090
you feel both things.

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:09.460
And it can be tempting to think like, I shouldn't
even have to say I didn't do it, but do say

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:10.920
that you didn't do it right.

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:14.480
Speaker 1: So like a reaction, like, why would
I do that?

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:17.460
That might be where we say, well, you're not
really denying it.

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:18.460
I mean, is that sort of.

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:20.180
Speaker 5: Nonresponsive to the question?

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:21.180
Speaker 1: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:22.180
Speaker 5: Yeah, yeah.

00:10:22.180 --> 00:10:27.690
The other observation that I learned from
them was that the biggest mistake we make

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:30.580
if we suspect something is going on is that
we don't ask the direct question.

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:31.580
Speaker 1: Right?

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:32.580
Speaker 5: Right.

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:35.340
And they're like, just ask the direct question
and get a direct answer one way or another.

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:37.990
And it's not that that proves anything.

00:10:37.990 --> 00:10:41.910
Like you can't be a human lie detector, but
is at least a flag to say like, oh, you might

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:43.260
want to look into it a little bit more.

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:44.260
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:47.500
In, in police interviewing techniques, which
I've read about a bit.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:50.610
One of the things that's often said and you
know, whether these things are always true

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:54.090
or mostly true, I guess it depends depends
on circumstances.

00:10:54.090 --> 00:11:00.670
But it's when you're interviewing a truly
innocent suspect, they will typically get

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:01.860
really mad.

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:07.730
As you continue to imply or suggest that they
did something, they will genuinely get mad

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:13.200
that they are being accused of this, whereas
guilty people will do some of the things you're

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:18.090
saying or kind of react in other ways because
it's genuinely infuriating to be accused of

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:20.130
something that you just you just didn't do.

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:21.510
Speaker 5: Yeah.

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:25.400
So so I think that's really important, which
is it's not that you don't feel the fury or

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:27.340
the indignant.

00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:33.160
So so being transparent about that even as
you are simultaneously being transparent about

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:34.660
what is not true.

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:35.660
Right.

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:40.290
Let me also say that this is related to most
difficult conversations, because we're often

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:43.090
having the conversation because something
is wrong.

00:11:43.090 --> 00:11:44.150
Yeah, right.

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:45.150
Something has gone wrong.

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:46.820
Somebody screwed something up.

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:48.260
Somebody did or said something.

00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:50.760
That's a problem.

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:54.560
And the and blame is a feature of most of
those conversations.

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:56.990
If it's not explicit, it's implicit.

00:11:56.990 --> 00:12:01.200
Like, you know, we need to talk about the
fact that you don't seem to know how to run.

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:02.200
Zoom right.

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:03.480
Use your microphone.

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:05.100
What is wrong with you?

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:12.070
And and if the purpose of the conversation
is actually to fix the problem, blame is often

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:18.840
not helpful because it's basically saying,
let's talk about why you're the problem here.

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:23.560
I'm the good guy and the victim and you are
the person who screwed it up and is incompetent.

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:24.560
Right.

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:27.790
And it's not surprising that not many people
really want to be part of that conversation.

00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:29.130
Like, no, thank you.

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:32.880
I don't want to be in that play with that
being cast in that role.

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:39.830
So so part of what we talk about, if you're
actually trying to problem solve is shifting

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:44.420
to talking about joint contribution like what
do we do or fail to do that got us where we're

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:48.580
at because I'll tell us what we need to change
if we're going to fix the problem and not

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:50.130
have it reoccur right.

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:54.280
Or should the same place next week, that's
a little bit different than the political

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:57.540
situation that you're talking about because
they're not actually trying to solve a problem.

00:12:57.540 --> 00:13:02.890
Speaker 1: No, it's usually about trying to
to appear as though you got one over on whoever

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:05.740
else you're speaking to or whatever the case
may be.

00:13:05.740 --> 00:13:10.070
And that's really at cross-purposes with much
of the productive ways to have these conversations.

00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:16.530
I mean, it's interesting, because 80% of political
speech in the United States is sort of structurally

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:19.490
not even designed to achieve anything.

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:23.941
Speaker 5: Well, what it's actually designed
to achieve is not a conversation with the

00:13:23.941 --> 00:13:24.941
other side.

00:13:24.941 --> 00:13:25.941
Right.

00:13:25.941 --> 00:13:27.320
It's a conversation with your face, right?

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:28.320
Yes.

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:29.320
So.

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:34.080
So you are saying it because you think it
is going to persuade or influence someone

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:35.080
or something.

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:41.120
It's just not what we're pretending it is
across the aisle or across party lines.

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:45.360
Speaker 1: When it comes to when you're in
the middle of some kind of tense exchange

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:49.320
and this could be a debate I'm having on my
show with someone who disagrees with me or

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:52.200
someone talking to their mom about whatever
they said or, you know, whatever the case

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:53.230
may be.

00:13:53.230 --> 00:13:57.430
Oftentimes, there's this the emotion kind
of blocks your ability to really think.

00:13:57.430 --> 00:14:00.700
And then after the fact, you often say, I
knew so many other things that I could have

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:02.440
said, but I didn't say, etc..

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:05.610
How how can you stay kind of level headed
during these exchanges?

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:07.700
Speaker 5: Well.

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:14.950
So I would I would distinguish between being
emotional.

00:14:14.950 --> 00:14:16.050
And sharing emotion.

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:17.140
Speaker 2: Hmm.

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:21.980
Speaker 5: Because I would argue that you
in those exchanges, you've actually got two

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:23.760
layers of things going on.

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:27.950
One is the debate that we're trying to have
or the issues that we're trying to.

00:14:27.950 --> 00:14:29.110
What?

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:30.110
Get one over on.

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:31.110
Yeah, you're right.

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:32.910
Or just better understand, sir.

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:35.720
Part of the advice is shift your purpose to.

00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:38.570
Can we understand why we see this differently?

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:41.800
If we can shift from proving that I'm right
to.

00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:44.320
It seems obvious that I'm right.

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:46.190
I'm just trying to understand why we see it
differently.

00:14:46.190 --> 00:14:47.950
That's actually a productive conversation.

00:14:47.950 --> 00:14:54.300
But you've got a second layer going on under
the surface, which is the emotions and relationship

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:58.800
layer that that is all about how I feel treated
in this exchange.

00:14:58.800 --> 00:14:59.800
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:04.440
One tool along those lines that I've been
told by a couple of different people is it

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:07.670
can be useful to say, okay, let's say the
topic is abortion.

00:15:07.670 --> 00:15:11.510
I'm pro-choice, I'm talking to someone who's
against abortion.

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:15.140
Ask the other person why they believe you
are pro-choice.

00:15:15.140 --> 00:15:19.170
To even see if they understand, even if they
disagree with your framework, how you came

00:15:19.170 --> 00:15:20.240
to the conclusion.

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:25.720
And that, number one, it's good for everybody
to have the knowledge, but just the exercise

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:30.780
of it might put the person in a position that
we're just by even saying it, they might at

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:33.790
least be more willing to understand your view
if not agree with it.

00:15:33.790 --> 00:15:35.960
Speaker 5: I think that that's right.

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:42.380
And what it highlights is that in any really
good conversation or attempt to influence,

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:46.550
by the way, you need two things.

00:15:46.550 --> 00:15:53.630
You need high quality assertion that you share
your view with clarity and thoughtfulness

00:15:53.630 --> 00:15:57.720
and etc., but you also actually need high
quality empathy.

00:15:57.720 --> 00:15:59.020
Mm hmm.

00:15:59.020 --> 00:16:02.970
That you are really listening generously and
deeply to each other.

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:04.160
To not to.

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:07.880
Because I think I'm going to agree with you,
but because in order to understand what might

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:11.800
be persuasive to you, I actually have to understand
what you think and why you think it.

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:12.800
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:17.360
Speaker 5: And then I can say, well, so here's
what it feels like is left out of your view

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:20.330
or that I worry about that it sounds like
you're not so worried about.

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:21.880
So talk to me about that.

00:16:21.880 --> 00:16:26.970
So so when we talk about high quality assertion,
it actually has to be rooted in deep empathy

00:16:26.970 --> 00:16:29.350
and and generous listening.

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:35.180
So the move that you just suggested is a clever
move, although it's basically backing the

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:38.390
other person into the corner to say, be empathetic
to me first.

00:16:38.390 --> 00:16:39.390
Right.

00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:42.440
And some people will take that and say like,
yeah, no, I actually think I can articulate

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:44.450
your view and that's helpful.

00:16:44.450 --> 00:16:48.040
But if that doesn't work, it may be because
you actually have to do it first.

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:49.040
Right.

00:16:49.040 --> 00:16:50.040
That you need to say so.

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:55.160
Let me see if I can describe what you're saying
and why you think what you think or feel the

00:16:55.160 --> 00:16:56.530
way that you feel.

00:16:56.530 --> 00:16:59.410
And then you can tell me what's left out of
that.

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:05.879
And then once they feel like you, you really
get it their view, then you're in a great

00:17:05.879 --> 00:17:12.000
position to basically say like, okay, so let
me say a little bit about why I see this differently.

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:15.589
And I would love it if you could see if you
can summarize it back to me, because this

00:17:15.589 --> 00:17:18.039
is just a complicated issue, right?

00:17:18.039 --> 00:17:23.230
And that sense of reciprocity, which is one
of the most robust findings in the literature.

00:17:23.230 --> 00:17:26.470
Like if you attack me, I'm going to attack
you back.

00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:33.960
But if if you're willing to listen to me and
consider genuinely, authentically why I think

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:38.559
what I think or feel how I feel, well, then
I almost feel an obligation to do the same.

00:17:38.559 --> 00:17:39.559
Speaker 1: Right.

00:17:39.559 --> 00:17:41.179
So that would be like with the abortion example.

00:17:41.179 --> 00:17:46.419
It might be, hey, listen, my my belief is
you're against it because you believe life

00:17:46.419 --> 00:17:50.429
starts at conception so that any abortion
is is like a murder.

00:17:50.429 --> 00:17:55.889
And but but but, Pippa, can you give me a
sense of why you think I am pro-choice?

00:17:55.889 --> 00:17:58.470
And so I'm kind of doing it first, in a sense.

00:17:58.470 --> 00:18:01.130
Speaker 5: Yes, you can.

00:18:01.130 --> 00:18:05.700
Or you might even tweet question was say,
can you give me a sense?

00:18:05.700 --> 00:18:09.830
And that's a genuine can you or do you feel
like you need more context from me in order

00:18:09.830 --> 00:18:11.090
to understand that?

00:18:11.090 --> 00:18:12.090
Speaker 1: Right.

00:18:12.090 --> 00:18:13.090
Right.

00:18:13.090 --> 00:18:14.090
Right.

00:18:14.090 --> 00:18:15.120
Speaker 5: Because there's the.

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:18.700
There's the general view of why people are
on each side of this issue.

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:23.360
But then there's the you view like why do
you, David, care about this?

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:24.360
Speaker 1: Right.

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:31.769
Speaker 5: And often around these really important
hot issues, political issues.

00:18:31.769 --> 00:18:35.350
One of the things that I hear is just differences
in what people worry most about.

00:18:35.350 --> 00:18:36.350
Speaker 2: Hmm.

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:43.460
Speaker 5: So someone who is pro-life worries
most about the life of the child.

00:18:43.460 --> 00:18:44.550
They would say child.

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:45.550
Speaker 1: Sure.

00:18:45.550 --> 00:18:47.179
Speaker 5: And murder.

00:18:47.179 --> 00:18:53.700
Someone who is pro-choice worries most about
other people making choices for individuals.

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:59.529
And what's interesting is that if we take
a couple steps back politically, Republicans

00:18:59.529 --> 00:19:06.059
often worry most about individual choice and
government interference in individual choices,

00:19:06.059 --> 00:19:08.860
which, of course, in abortion, that's flipped.

00:19:08.860 --> 00:19:10.130
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:19:10.130 --> 00:19:17.250
Speaker 5: Whereas Democrats often worry most
about the voices of those who are oppressed

00:19:17.250 --> 00:19:19.870
and not in the majority and equity.

00:19:19.870 --> 00:19:20.870
Right.

00:19:20.870 --> 00:19:22.139
Or fairness.

00:19:22.139 --> 00:19:28.639
And so often one of the the ears I have on
the conversation is whatever it is we're talking

00:19:28.639 --> 00:19:32.179
about, what is it that each of us is worried
most about?

00:19:32.179 --> 00:19:35.510
And in some ways, I don't have to worry about
what you're worried about because I know that

00:19:35.510 --> 00:19:36.510
you'll take care of it.

00:19:36.510 --> 00:19:37.510
Speaker 1: Right.

00:19:37.510 --> 00:19:41.980
Speaker 5: If there wasn't anybody protecting
individual autonomy or choice from government,

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:42.980
I would have to worry about it.

00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:46.779
But I can rely on you guys to worry about
it still so I can worry about something else

00:19:46.779 --> 00:19:50.269
that actually matters to us as a society as
well.

00:19:50.269 --> 00:19:52.169
Speaker 1: Super interesting.

00:19:52.169 --> 00:19:57.330
We've been speaking with Harvard Law School
professor Sheila Heen, coauthor of The New

00:19:57.330 --> 00:20:01.630
York Times bestsellers Difficult Conversations,
as well as thanks for the feedback.

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:02.820
Really appreciate your time today.

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:04.030
Thank you so much.

00:20:04.030 --> 00:20:04.899
Speaker 5: Thank you.

